if you wanted to become the best at guerrilla warfare in human history, what would be the list of terms and definitions to know?
====================================

If your goal were to become one of the most knowledgeable people in human history about guerrilla warfare, I would approach it as mastering an entire discipline—not simply learning weapons, ambushes, or unconventional combat.

You would need to understand military history, irregular warfare, insurgency, counterinsurgency, intelligence, political strategy, logistics, psychology, geography, and the relationship between military force and civilian populations.

I can build you a comprehensive guerrilla warfare terminology encyclopedia, focused on historical, strategic, analytical, and defensive understanding rather than instructions for carrying out attacks.

# The Guerrilla Warfare Master Vocabulary

## 1. Foundational concepts

| Term | Definition |
| --- | --- |
| Guerrilla Warfare | Irregular warfare conducted by smaller forces against a more powerful opponent. |
| Irregular Warfare (IW) | Conflict involving state or non-state actors using methods outside conventional military operations. |
| Asymmetric Warfare | Conflict between opponents with substantially different capabilities, resources, or strategies. |
| Unconventional Warfare (UW) | Military activities supporting an organized resistance movement against an occupying or controlling power. |
| Insurgency | An organized movement seeking to challenge or overthrow an established authority. |
| Counterinsurgency (COIN) | Political, military, and social efforts to defeat an insurgency. |
| Rebellion | Organized resistance against an established government or authority. |
| Revolution | Fundamental transformation of a political or social system. |
| Resistance Movement | An organized effort opposing occupation, domination, or authoritarian control. |
| Partisan Warfare | Irregular warfare conducted by organized fighters, often behind enemy-controlled territory. |
| People's War | A theory of protracted conflict emphasizing political mobilization and popular support. |
| Hybrid Warfare | A combination of conventional, irregular, informational, economic, and other forms of conflict. |
| Protracted War | A conflict deliberately extended over a long period. |
| Low-Intensity Conflict | A form of political-military confrontation below conventional large-scale warfare. |
| Proxy Warfare | Conflict in which an outside power supports another actor to pursue strategic objectives. |

