| Operational Art | Connecting military operations to broader strategic objectives. |
| Grand Strategy | Coordinating political, military, economic, and diplomatic resources toward national objectives. |
| Attrition | Gradually weakening an opponent through cumulative losses. |
| War of Exhaustion | A strategy centered on exhausting an opponent's resources, resolve, or political willingness. |
| Maneuver Warfare | A military philosophy emphasizing disruption of an opponent's ability to respond coherently. |
| Initiative | The ability to influence the timing, direction, and character of events. |
| Freedom of Action | The ability to make and execute decisions without unacceptable external restrictions. |
| Escalation | An increase in the intensity, scope, or severity of conflict. |
| Deterrence | Preventing an adversary from taking an action by influencing its cost-benefit calculations. |
| Coercion | Using pressure or threatened consequences to influence another actor's decisions. |
| Strategic Depth | The geographic, political, economic, or organizational resources available to sustain a conflict. |
| Culminating Point | The stage at which an operation or campaign can no longer sustain its current momentum. |
| Theory of Victory | An explanation of how a particular strategy is expected to produce political success. |

